= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Integrate each conservative <ideal magnetohydrodynamics> law through a thin pillbox around the stationary shock. Mass conservation gives
$$
[\rho u_x]=0.
$$
The MHD momentum-flux tensor is
$$
\rho\mathbf u\mathbf u
+\left(p+\frac{B^2}{2\mu_0}\right)I
-\frac{\mathbf B\mathbf B}{\mu_0}.
$$
Its normal and tangential components give
$$
\left[\rho u_x^2+\Pi-\frac{B_x^2}{\mu_0}\right]=0,
\qquad
\left[\rho u_xu_y-\frac{B_xB_y}{\mu_0}\right]=0,
$$
where $\Pi=p+B^2/(2\mu_0)$ is total pressure. The solenoidal condition gives $[B_x]=0$, while steady <Faraday's law> gives continuity of tangential electric field,
$$
[-E_z]=[u_xB_y-u_yB_x]=0.
$$
Finally, material energy flux plus the normal <Poynting vector> component gives
$$
\left[\rho u_x\left(\frac12u^2+h\right)
-\frac{E_zB_y}{\mu_0}\right]=0,
$$
where $h$ is <Enthalpy>[specific enthalpy] per unit mass.

Gravity is a bounded volume force, so its integral across a shock whose thickness tends to zero vanishes. Viscosity and resistivity inside the layer may produce entropy; consequently entropy flux need not be equal on both sides, although the second law requires nonnegative net entropy production.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The quantities $B_x$ and $u_xB_y-u_yB_x=-E_z$ are each constant across the shock. A single tangential Galilean boost therefore changes the common $E_z$ to zero on both sides. This is the <de Hoffmann–Teller frame>. Ideal MHD then gives
$$
\mathbf u_i\times\mathbf B_i=0,
\qquad
\boxed{\mathbf u_i\parallel\mathbf B_i}.
$$

Let $j=\rho u_x$ and retain the common $B_x$. The independent jump conditions simplify to
$$
[j]=0,
\qquad [B_x]=0,
$$
$$
\left[\rho u_x^2+p+\frac{B_y^2}{2\mu_0}\right]=0,
$$
$$
\left[\rho u_xu_y-\frac{B_xB_y}{\mu_0}\right]=0,
$$
$$
\left[\frac12u^2+h\right]=0,
\qquad
\frac{u_y}{u_x}=\frac{B_y}{B_x}.
$$
Terms involving the common $B_x^2$ cancel from normal momentum, and the electromagnetic term vanishes from energy because $E_z=0$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In the aligned frame, define the <Alfvén number>
$$
A_i^2=\frac{u_i^2}{v_{Ai}^2}
=\frac{\mu_0\rho_i u_{xi}^2}{B_x^2},
$$
where the second equality uses $\mathbf u_i\parallel\mathbf B_i$. Since $j=\rho_i u_{xi}$ and $B_x$ are common,
$$
A_i^2=\frac{\mu_0j u_{xi}}{B_x^2}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac{\rho_2}{\rho_1}
=\frac{u_{x1}}{u_{x2}}
=\frac{A_1^2}{A_2^2}}.
$$

Using alignment in the tangential momentum flux gives
$$
\rho u_xu_y-\frac{B_xB_y}{\mu_0}
=\frac{B_xB_y}{\mu_0}(A^2-1).
$$
Its continuity then yields
$$
B_{y2}(A_2^2-1)=B_{y1}(A_1^2-1),
$$
or
$$
\boxed{\frac{B_{y2}}{B_{y1}}
=\frac{A_1^2-1}{A_2^2-1}}
$$
whenever the ratios are determinate.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

If $B_{y1}=B_{y2}=0$, alignment also gives $u_{y1}=u_{y2}=0$. The common normal magnetic field contributes the same constant magnetic stress on both sides and carries no Poynting flux in the aligned frame. The remaining mass, normal-momentum, and energy conditions are therefore exactly
$$
[\rho u_x]=0,
\qquad
[\rho u_x^2+p]=0,
\qquad
\left[\frac12u_x^2+h\right]=0,
$$
which are the <Rankine-Hugoniot conditions for a perfect gas>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $B_{y2}=0$ but $B_{y1}\ne0$, tangential momentum continuity in the form
$$
B_{y2}(A_2^2-1)=B_{y1}(A_1^2-1)
$$
forces $A_1^2=1$. Since the normal components are positive,
$$
\boxed{A_1=1},
$$
so the upstream velocity equals the upstream <Alfvén speed>. This is the limiting switch-off shock condition.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= e
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Take $B_{y2}=-B_{y1}\ne0$ and choose
$$
A_1=A_2=1.
$$
Then both tangential momentum fluxes vanish, so that jump condition is satisfied. The density relation from part (c) gives $\rho_2=\rho_1$, and mass conservation gives $u_{x2}=u_{x1}$. Alignment makes $u_{y2}=-u_{y1}$, so $u_2^2=u_1^2$.

Because $B_{y2}^2=B_{y1}^2$, normal momentum now gives
$$
\boxed{p_2=p_1}.
$$
The energy condition is then automatic. Thus density and pressure are continuous while the tangential magnetic field and velocity reverse direction. This is a $180^\circ$ <rotational discontinuity in magnetohydrodynamics>, carrying no compression or entropy jump.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

