= Solution
{parent=a}

The representation
$$
\mathbf B_p=\nabla\psi\times\nabla\phi
$$
gives
$$
B_r=-\frac1r\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial z},
\qquad
B_z=\frac1r\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial r}.
$$
Hence $\mathbf B\mathbin\cdot\nabla\psi=0$: the level sets of the <poloidal magnetic flux function> are magnetic surfaces. Moreover, the magnetic flux through a circular disc is
$$
\int_0^r2\pi r'B_z(r',z)\,dr'
=2\pi[\psi(r,z)-\psi(0,z)].
$$
Thus $2\pi\psi$ is the enclosed poloidal magnetic flux up to an additive axial reference.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The flux-function form solves $\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B=0$ identically. With $k=k(\psi)$, the poloidal relation $\rho\mathbf u_p=k\mathbf B_p$ and axisymmetry solve steady mass conservation. The ideal induction equation is solved by the velocity representation with the <field-line angular velocity> $\omega=\omega(\psi)$. The azimuthal momentum equation integrates once to the <magnetohydrodynamic angular-momentum invariant> $\ell=\ell(\psi)$.

What remains is the pressure or entropy equation and the two poloidal components of momentum. Projecting poloidal momentum along a field line produces the Bernoulli invariant in part (c); projecting across magnetic surfaces produces the transfield or Grad-Shafranov equation that determines their shape. An equation of state and boundary conditions complete the problem. The stellar gravitational potential is prescribed, so no self-gravity Poisson equation remains to solve.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Although the <Lorentz force> can exchange energy between poloidal and toroidal motion, it does no net work in the frame rotating with a magnetic surface. Dotting steady momentum with the poloidal field and using the mass-loading, isorotation, and angular-momentum invariants combines the magnetic work with the toroidal kinetic term to give
$$
\mathbf B_p\mathbin\cdot\nabla\widetilde\epsilon=0.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\epsilon(\psi)
=\frac12u_p^2+\frac12(u_\phi-r\omega)^2
+\Phi_{cg}+h}
$$
is constant on each magnetic surface. Here
$$
\boxed{\Phi_{cg}=\Phi-\frac12\omega^2r^2}
$$
is the gravitational potential plus the centrifugal potential in the frame corotating at the field-line angular velocity. This is the modified <Bernoulli function> of the wind.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Define the poloidal <Alfvén number> by
$$
\boxed{A^2=\frac{\mu_0\rho u_p^2}{B_p^2}
=\frac{\mu_0k^2}{\rho}},
$$
where $u_p=kB_p/\rho$. Thus, along a given field line,
$$
\boxed{\rho=\frac{\mu_0k^2}{A^2}};
$$
density decreases as the Alfvén number grows.

The azimuthal velocity representation gives
$$
u_\phi=r\omega+\frac{kB_\phi}{\rho}.
$$
Substitution into $\ell=ru_\phi-rB_\phi/(\mu_0k)$ yields
$$
\ell=r^2\omega+\frac{rB_\phi}{\mu_0k}(A^2-1).
$$
At the <Alfvén surface>, $A^2=1$. Smoothness requires the numerator of the solution for $B_\phi$ to vanish there, so
$$
\ell=\omega r_a^2.
$$
Solving away from the surface then gives
$$
\boxed{B_\phi
=\frac{\mu_0k\omega}{r}
\frac{r^2-r_a^2}{1-A^2}}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= e
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Ideal flux freezing anchors a field line in the highly conducting disc, so its <field-line angular velocity> naturally equals the angular velocity of its footpoint. A circular orbit in the central potential is Keplerian:
$$
\omega(\psi)=\Omega_0
=\sqrt{\frac{GM}{r_0^3}}.
$$

Put $r=r_0+\delta r$ and expand
$$
\Phi_{cg}=-\frac{GM}{\sqrt{r^2+z^2}}
-\frac12\Omega_0^2r^2.
$$
The linear radial term vanishes because $GM/r_0^2=\Omega_0^2r_0$. The radial and vertical second derivatives are respectively $-3\Omega_0^2$ and $\Omega_0^2$, so
$$
\boxed{\Phi_{cg}
\simeq\text{constant}
+\frac12\Omega_0^2[z^2-3(r-r_0)^2]}.
$$

If the field line makes angle $\theta$ from the vertical, then locally $\delta r=z\tan\theta$. Its quadratic potential change is
$$
\frac12\Omega_0^2z^2(1-3\tan^2\theta),
$$
which is negative exactly when
$$
\boxed{\theta>30^\circ}.
$$
This is the local <magnetocentrifugal acceleration> launching criterion.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= f
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The velocity representation gives
$$
u_\phi-r\omega=\frac{kB_\phi}{\rho},
\qquad
u_p=\frac{kB_p}{\rho}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{R=\frac{|u_\phi-r\omega|}{u_p}
=\frac{|B_\phi|}{B_p}}.
$$
In the launching region, $r\simeq r_0\ll r_a$ and $A\ll1$, so part (d) gives
$$
|B_\phi|\simeq\frac{\mu_0k\Omega_0r_a^2}{r_0}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{R\simeq\widetilde R
=\frac{r_a^2\Omega_0\mu_0k}{r_0B_p}}.
$$

The modified Bernoulli kinetic energy is
$$
\frac12u_p^2+\frac12(u_\phi-r\omega)^2
=\frac12(1+R^2)u_p^2.
$$
Thus a decrease in $\Phi_{cg}$ is shared between poloidal acceleration and relative toroidal motion. If $R$ is approximately constant, the same potential drop produces a poloidal kinetic increase smaller by the factor $1/(1+R^2)$ than the estimate that neglects the toroidal term. If $R$ varies strongly, that term can absorb or return energy, so monotonic decrease of $\Phi_{cg}$ alone no longer proves an equally direct increase of $u_p^2/2$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.
